.}.\n\nA cube of side 3 inches has a cube of side 1 inch cut from each corner. A cube of side 2 inches is then inserted in each corner. What is the number of square inches in the surface area of the resulting solid?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"73ad80949f0efd118fbf2a7fbf78077d4c5668835efe6b443b70697223d23658"},"seconds":11.266100645065308,"seed":1104040656492022349,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the surface area of the resulting solid, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the surface area of the original cube:**\n   The original cube has a side length of 3 inches. The surface area \\(A_{\\text{original}}\\) of a cube is given by:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{original}} = 6 \\times \\text{side}^2 = 6 \\times 3^2 = 6 \\times 9 = 54 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the effect of cutting out smaller cubes from each corner:**\n   When a cube of side 1 inch is cut from each of the 8 corners, each cut removes a 1-inch by 1-inch square from the surface of the original cube for each of the 3 faces that meet at the corner. However, these cuts also create new faces on the resulting solid. Each 1-inch cube cut out removes 3 square inches of surface area (one from each of the three faces it was attached to) and adds 3 new square inches of surface area (one for each of the three new faces created by the cut). Therefore, each cut does not change the total surface area of the solid. Since there are 8 such cuts, the total surface area remains:\n   \\[\n   54 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the effect of inserting larger cubes into each corner:**\n   When a cube of side 2 inches is inserted into each of the 8 corners, each new cube adds 4 new square inches of surface area (since a 2-inch cube has 6 faces but 2 of these faces are internal and do not contribute to the external surface area). Therefore, each insertion adds \\(4 \\times 8 = 32\\) square inches to the surface area. The total surface area of the resulting solid is:\n   \\[\n   54 + 32 = 86 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of square inches in the surface area of the resulting solid is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{86}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{86}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:294"}
